course: dictionary
lesson: 5954
title: Words from a different world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A matador is a word from Spanish, and the sentences around it give clues. Read this: «Bulls cannot see red well; it is the movement of the matador's cape that makes them charge.» The matador waves a cape and the bull runs at it, so a matador must be the bullfighter who waves the cape.
explain: Clues can also tell you about history and feelings. Bullfighting is a very old tradition, and many people now think it is cruel to animals, so some places have banned it. Stories often show a matador as brave, or as showing off. The words around the word help you decide which.
explain: The word itself can tell you a story. Matador comes from Spanish and means "killer", and the matador's job is to try to kill the bull with a sword, so the name fits. Notice the phrase «like a matador», which means dodging neatly, like a bullfighter swerving past a charge.
fact: A matador is the main bullfighter in a Spanish-style bullfight.
fact: A matador tries to kill the bull with a sword.
fact: Matadors wear a sparkly "suit of lights" and wave a red cape called a muleta.
fact: Bulls are red-green colour-blind, so it is the movement of the cape that makes them charge, not its colour.
fact: "Matador" comes from Spanish and means "killer".
exercise: In «Bulls cannot see red well; it is the movement of the matador's cape that makes them charge», what makes the bull charge? | *The movement of the cape | The matador's shouting | The colour of the cape | The sound of the crowd
exercise: What does the Spanish word matador mean? | Bull | Dance | Sword | *Killer
exercise: What is the matador's main job in a bullfight? | Feeding the bull | Watching from the stands | *Trying to kill the bull with a sword | Selling tickets
test: What is the red cloth a matador waves called? | Sword | Suit of lights | *Muleta | Killer
test: What does the phrase «like a matador» mean? | Being afraid of animals | *Dodging neatly | To fight very slowly | To wave a red cloth
exam: Why do many people today oppose bullfighting? | It is too quick to watch | *They think it is cruel to animals | Bulls are too small to fight | They want more matadors
